From bd4b6b13c8120f50fd0fcea6faa93364957f424f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Enrico Bartky <enrico.bartky@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 4 Aug 2026 10:33:46 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 2/2] Specialize a promoted function's monomorphic reads, item
 4-2b

The item asked for "monomorphic property access to a shape check plus direct
slot read". What was missing was not the codegen - 4-3b built the in-method
branch - but a way for the tier-2 compile to ADDRESS tier-1's feedback. A
recompile re-parses the source, so every property read it emitted allocated a
fresh inline-cache site: promoting a function silently threw away every warm
cache it had, and the original sites' indices were nowhere, so nothing could ask
what they had seen.

Tier-1 now records the half-open range of read sites its body compile allocated
and tier-2 hands those same indices back out in emission order. That carries the
warm caches across promotion and makes item 4-1's per-site feedback addressable.
The mapping is ordinal and deliberately not trusted: the site counter is
process-wide, so two threads compiling at once is enough to slide it, and the
emitted guard therefore compares the key it was built for against the key
actually read. A slipped mapping fails its guard and falls back, so the mapping
is a performance heuristic and never a correctness dependency.

A site whose whole history is one shape resolving one key to one own slot is
emitted through SpeculationBuilder.Guarded as a key compare, a type test, a
shape-id compare against a literal and a slot load at a literal index, with the
cached get as the fallback arm. This is 4-3b's first JavaScript-level consumer
and it needs the guarantee that facility exists for - the receiver is evaluated
exactly once, so f().x does not run f() twice.

Counted exactly rather than argued: a specialized read records no cache hit, so
cacheHits(tiered) - cacheHits(specializing) is the number of executed reads
taken off the cache path, and cache MISSES come out identical in six of seven
suites. 16 943 490 of 37 871 908 reads, 44.74%, carried by 1 130 sites, with
156 guard misses and 30 poisoned sites - the monomorphism holds well past the
promotion point.

Each such read is 0.818x (46.83 -> 37.12 ns per iteration, six pairs,
0.778-0.879) on a probe whose body is one read in a loop. The suite wall clock
does not move: 0.9947 against a feedback-on control, which is the arm that
matters - a first two-arm run read DeltaBlue's 1.232 as the specialization's
cost when it is the cost of collecting feedback (1.0249 overall). That is
arithmetic rather than failure: 16.9 M x 9.7 ns is 164 ms of a 19.7 s run,
0.83%, under a +/-2% floor. It also bounds the phase - the whole read path is at
most ~9% of Octane's execution time here and the whole call path at most ~5.5%.

Off by default. SpecializeFromTypeFeedback gates the emission on the plan rather
than on whether feedback happens to be recording, so collection and consumption
are independently controllable, which is what made the three-arm measurement
expressible at all.

Adds SpecializingTierTests (16 cases) and --specializing-read-probe. Repository
suite: 7 823 tests across 13 projects, 0 failures.
